    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Store the last index of each character.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction longestUniqueSubstringLength(value: string): number {\n  const lastSeen = new Map<string, number>();\n  let left = 0;\n  let best = 0;\n\n  for (let right = 0; right < value.length; right++) {\n    const ch = value[right]!;\n    const previous = lastSeen.get(ch);\n\n    if (previous !== undefined && previous >= left) {\n      left = previous + 1;\n    }\n\n    lastSeen.set(ch, right);\n    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n  }\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction longestUniqueSubstringUsingSet(value: string): number {\n  const window = new Set<string>();\n  let left = 0;\n  let best = 0;\n\n  for (let right = 0; right < value.length; right++) {\n    while (window.has(value[right]!)) {\n      window.delete(value[left]!);\n      left++;\n    }\n\n    window.add(value[right]!);\n    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n  }\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n\"abcabcbb\"\nbest window = \"abc\" → 3.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nBoth approaches are O(n) time.\nSpace is O(min(n, alphabet size)) for stored characters.",
