    expectedAnswer: "A monotonic deque keeps only useful candidates inside a sliding window. For maximum values, values decrease from front to back, and expired indexes are removed from the front.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Example: maximum of every k-sized window.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction maxSlidingWindow(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  k: number,\n): number[] {\n  if (k <= 0 || k > nums.length) return [];\n\n  const deque: number[] = [];\n  let front = 0;\n  const result: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {\n    // Remove indexes outside the window.\n    if (front < deque.length && deque[front]! <= right - k) {\n      front++;\n    }\n\n    // Keep deque values decreasing.\n    while (\n      deque.length > front &&\n      nums[deque[deque.length - 1]!]! <= nums[right]!\n    ) {\n      deque.pop();\n    }\n\n    deque.push(right);\n\n    if (right >= k - 1) {\n      result.push(nums[deque[front]!]!);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction maxSlidingWindowUsingSlice(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  k: number,\n): number[] {\n  const result: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i + k <= nums.length; i++) {\n    result.push(Math.max(...nums.slice(i, i + k)));\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n[1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7], k=3\n→ [3,3,5,5,6,7].\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nMonotonic deque O(n) time, O(k) auxiliary space.\nSlice/Math.max baseline is O(nk).",
